<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus (consul 177 BCE)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 217–154 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus was a Roman general and statesman, father of the reformers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius-gracchus">Tiberius Gracchus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-gracchus">Gaius Gracchus</a>. As governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania-citerior">Hispania Citerior</a> from 180 to 178 BCE he ended the First Celtiberian War with treaties that kept peace in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> for a generation, and founded the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gracchurris">Gracchurris</a>. He later twice served as consul.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gracchus was a plebeian of the well-connected gens Sempronia; his grandfather, or possibly his father, was the Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus who was consul in 215 and 213 BCE. Little is known of his youth. He may be the same man as the augur of that name who served from 204 to 174 BCE. He served with the Scipios in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, where he was sent as an envoy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-macedon">Philip V of Macedon</a> to negotiate safe passage to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a>.</p><p>As tribune of the plebs in 184 BCE, or possibly 187 BCE, he used his veto to save <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio Africanus</a> from prosecution and Lucius Cornelius Scipio Asiagenes from prison, though he was no political ally of theirs. As curule aedile in 182 BCE he staged games so lavish that the senate capped such expenses to spare <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s Italian allies and provinces.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After his command in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, where he claimed to have destroyed three hundred cities, almost certainly an exaggeration, he and his colleague Lucius Postumius Albinus triumphed and presented some 60 thousand pounds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>. As consul in 177 BCE with Gaius Claudius Pulcher he was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, where he waged two campaigns against the Ilienses and the Balari. Returning in 175 BCE, he claimed some 80 thousand Sardinians killed or captured and triumphed for the second time.</p><p>As censor from 169 BCE, again with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a>, he was strict enough to provoke a prosecution of his colleague, registered all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a> in a single urban tribe and built the Basilica Sempronia in the Forum. In his second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">consulship</a>, in 163 BCE, he made an error in the auspices at the elections for 162 BCE; when he reported it, the new consuls, among them his brother-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-cornelius-scipio-nasica-corculum">Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica Corculum</a>, had to resign. He also led embassies to the eastern kingdoms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He married Cornelia, the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio Africanus</a>, a match that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>, following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, places after Africanus's death. She bore him twelve children, of whom three reached adulthood: Sempronia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius-gracchus">Tiberius Gracchus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-gracchus">Gaius Gracchus</a>. Plutarch tells that soothsayers warned him over two snakes found in his bedchamber, and that he killed the male, choosing his own death over hers, and died soon after. Cornelia refused further marriages, among them an offer from Ptolemy VIII, and raised their sons, whose fame has since overshadowed his own.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus (consul 177 BC)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rome reached their land in the early 2nd century BCE: Marcus Fulvius Nobilior won a battle near Toletum in 193 BCE and took the town in 192 BCE, and <b>Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus</b> pacified the region by 179 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/carpetani">Carpetani</a></li><li><blockquote>Cato crushed the north-eastern revolt in 195 BCE, and <b>Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus</b> made treaties with the Celtiberians in 179 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/hispania-citerior">Hispania Citerior</a></li><li><blockquote>In 181 BCE they took part in the First Celtiberian War, which ended with the treaty of <b>Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus</b> in 179 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/lusones">Lusones</a></li><li><blockquote>When Segeda enlarged its walls, it forced the Titti to settle there, which Rome treated as a breach of the treaty of <b>Gracchus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/titti">Titti</a></li></ul></section>
